 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:45+08:00",
 "answer": "If you mean **Singapore’s PSI**, the two labels can both be right. The reported PSI reflects pollutant levels averaged over **24 hours**, so it changes slowly. Your app may be showing a more recent PM₂.₅ reading or an AQI calculated on a different scale. Air quality can worsen quickly while the 24-hour PSI still says “moderate.” ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_little-text-block))\n\nFor a decision about **going outside now**, check the **1-hour PM₂.₅ reading** for your area alongside the PSI and its advisory. Also check whether the app says *AQI* rather than *PSI*, and whether both readings cover the same location and time. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_little-text-block))\n\nIf you tell me the app name and the two numbers, I can help pinpoint the difference.",
